# Local data
applications.json
*.local.json

# One working folder per application: the CV that was sent, plus the form questions and
# answers — salary expectations and personal reasoning. Not applications.json (the store).
applications/

# The day's review sheet and its data sidecar. Regenerated by every scrape, and
# superseded by jobtrack + applications/LOG.md once picked — nothing here is a record.
TODAY_SCRAPING.md
TODAY_SCRAPING.json

# Ashby boards found by `jobs.discover`, with the freshness each scrape records. Rewritten
# daily and rebuilt by the next discover run if lost.
jobs/ashby_boards.json
jobs/discover.log
jobs/.discover.lock

# Personal CV data — contains phone, email and credential IDs. One profile per
# language, and both are personal. The builders ARE tracked; the data and the
# rendered documents are not. A new user generates their own profile.json with
# `python cv/init.py`, so there is nothing to check in here.
cv/profile.json
cv/fr/profile_fr.json
cv/out/
cv/archive/
src/CVs/
CVs/

# Your decision criteria: which jobs are worth applying to, and how the CV may be
# written. Personal in the same way profile.json is — they encode one person's
# constraints and would rank someone else's leads wrongly. The `.example.md`
# alongside each one IS tracked; copy it and rewrite. `/jobhunt` reads these.
jobs/priorities.md
cv/rules.md
# What the scrape ranks for - home country, work authorization, target roles. Personal for
# the same reason; settings.example.json beside it is tracked.
jobs/settings.json

